{"name":"Dry Seedlings","hex":"#c7dc68","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Darai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free from or lacking moisture.","Unable to produce a liquid, as water,  oil, or  milk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dry"},{"term":"seedlings","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of seedling"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seedlings"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seedling","blocks":[{"text":"A seedling is a young sporophyte developing out of a plant embryo from a seed. Seedling development starts with germination of the seed. A typical young seedling consists of three main parts: the radicle, the hypocotyl, and the cotyledons. The two classes of flowering plants (angiosperms) are distinguished by their numbers of seed leaves: monocotyledons (monocots) have one blade-shaped cotyledon, whereas dicotyledons (dicots) possess two round cotyledons. Gymnosperms are more varied. For example, pine seedlings have up to eight cotyledons. The seedlings of some flowering plants have no cotyledons at all. These are said to be acotyledons."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Monocot_vs_dicot_crop_Pengo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seedling"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Seedling of a Scots pine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG/500px-Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG/1280px-Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG/500px-Nandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Seedling of Nandina domestica (a dicot) showing two green cotyledon leaves, and the first \"true\" leaf with its distinct leaflets and red-green color.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANandina_domestica-Clapiers-4050~2015_03_29.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg/250px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg/500px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg/250px-Scots_pine_seedling_in_natural_environment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.